\midrule
Qwen-base   & 2/2 & $.6288$ $[.620,\,.638]$ & $.6258$ $[.616,\,.635]$ & $-.0031$ \\
Pythia-1.4B  & 2/2 & $.8840$ $[.878,\,.890]$ & $.8830$ $[.876,\,.889]$ & $-.0010$ \\
LLaMA-8B     & 2/2 & $.6075$ $[.598,\,.617]$ & $.6074$ $[.598,\,.617]$ & $-.0001$ \\
Mistral-7B   & 1/1 & $.5902$ $[.580,\,.600]$ & $.5903$ $[.580,\,.600]$ & $+.0002$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

All four configurations preserve their $\Kempirical$ under 14D, with negligible AUC change ($|\Delta| \leq 0.003$).
The self\_BLEU feature contributes minimally to the depth boundary; $\Kempirical{=}2$ is driven by surprisal and entropy features rather than lexical repetition statistics.

\subsection{Wikipedia Domain: Scorer Confound Resolution}
\label{sec:wiki_domain}

Under same-family scoring (Qwen-1.5B scorer), Wikipedia $\Kempirical$ appeared to exhibit model-family dependence: Qwen generators (1.5B and 7B) yielded $\Kempirical{=}2$, while all four non-Qwen models yielded $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Table~\ref{tab:wiki_domain}).
A scorer confound ablation resolves this apparent split.
Rescoring the same Qwen-7B Wikipedia text with Pythia-1.4B reduces 1v2 $\auc$ from $0.611$ to $0.587$ ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp), dropping $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1.
With the scorer confound controlled, all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia, and the domain ordering simplifies to: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Wikipedia pairwise $\auc$ across 6 models. $^\dagger$Qwen rows use same-family scorer (Qwen-1.5B); cross-family scoring (Pythia-1.4B) reduces Qwen-7B to $\Kempirical{=}1$ ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp), resolving the apparent family split.}
\label{tab:wiki_domain}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{0v1} & \textbf{1v2} & \textbf{2v3} & $\Kempirical$ & \textbf{Scorer} \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B (base) & Qwen & ---  & .619 & .550 & 2$^\dagger$ & Qwen-1.5B \\
Qwen-1.5B (base) & Qwen & ---  & .599 & ---  & 1 & Pythia-1.4B \\
Qwen-7B          & Qwen & .967 & .611 & .556 & 2$^\dagger$ & Qwen-1.5B \\
Qwen-7B          & Qwen & ---  & .587 & ---  & 1 & Pythia-1.4B \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B   & non-Qwen & .975 & .585 & .545 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
OLMo-1B       & non-Qwen & .993 & .499 & .501 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B  & non-Qwen & .932 & .595 & .539 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
Mistral-7B    & non-Qwen & .961 & .555 & .524 & 1 & Qwen-1.5B \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Same-family scorer; cross-family scoring yields $\Kempirical{=}1$.}
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-fold transparency.}
For Qwen-1.5B, cross-family scoring (Pythia-1.4B) yields $\auc$(1v2) $= 0.5994$, with per-fold values $[0.596, 0.606, 0.600, 0.603, 0.592]$ (2/5 folds marginally above $\theta = 0.60$).
The aggregate mean falls below $\theta$, confirming $\Kempirical{=}1$.
For Qwen-7B, the corresponding $\auc$ drops further to $0.587$.

\subsection{ArXiv Cross-Scorer Bootstrap CIs}
\label{sec:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Bootstrap 95\% CI for arXiv cross-scorer (Pythia-1.4B) 2v3 boundary $\auc$ (10{,}000 resamples). Point estimates classify 3/4 generators as $\Kempirical{=}3$; strict CI lower-bound criterion confirms 2/4.}
\label{tab:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Generator} & \textbf{2v3 $\auc$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{CI lower $> \theta$} \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B    & 0.632 & $[0.617,\,0.646]$ & Yes \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & 0.620 & $[0.605,\,0.636]$ & Yes (0.5\,pp margin) \\
Mistral-7B   & 0.608 & $[0.593,\,0.622]$ & No \\
Qwen-7B      & 0.599 & $[0.584,\,0.613]$ & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{JSD Decay Analysis}
\label{sec:jsd_extended}

The token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two distinct decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}).
For C4 web text, 0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$ across all 8 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$ (median cliff ratio ${\sim}$15$\times$), and subsequent pairs converge to a noise floor of ${\sim}$0.003--0.005.
Fitting $\jsd(k, k{+}1) \propto e^{-\lambda k}$ yields $\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$ for C4 (mean ${\approx}\,2.9$), consistent with rapid mixing of $G_\delta$ toward its stationary distribution.
These decay rates represent qualitative characterizations from 5-point curves rather than precise estimates.
The empirical decay rate $\lambda$ is consistent within each domain but varies across domains: C4 yields $\lambda \approx 2.9$, Wikipedia $\lambda \approx 3.1$ (excluding OLMo, whose $\lambda = 6.3$ reflects a capacity-limited collapse: 0v1 $\jsd = 0.169$ with near-complete information loss at the first step, making the exponential fit unreliable), and arXiv $\lambda \approx 1.0$.
This cross-model consistency indicates that the contraction rate of $G_\delta$ is governed primarily by the structure of the input domain rather than the model architecture.
ArXiv text decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower than C4 ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$), with a correspondingly higher convergence floor (${\sim}$0.007--0.009 vs.\ ${\sim}$0.003--0.004).
Formulaic structural features (LaTeX formatting, citation patterns, section templates) degrade more slowly under autoregressive generation and supply persistent depth-distinguishing signal, explaining the elevated $\Kempirical{=}3$.
The retrain-chain exhibits a fundamentally different profile: $\lambda = 0.285$ with a cliff ratio of only 1.31$\times$ and no sharp drop between adjacent pairs.
This gradual, sustained divergence reflects cumulative model-adaptation artifacts rather than text-intrinsic information loss, further supporting the distinction between prompt-chain and retrain-chain $\Kempirical$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:boundary_extended}).

\subsection{Boundary Cases and Scale Effect}
\label{sec:boundary_extended}

Of 9 tested model configurations on C4, 8 yield $\Kempirical{=}2$; Mistral-7B is the sole sub-2 exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$; 1v2 $\auc = 0.594$, CI $[0.584, 0.603]$), validating $\theta{=}0.60$ as an effective discriminator (gap: $0.594$ vs.\ $0.611$--$0.696$).
Mistral's cross-domain profile reinforces the domain-governed interpretation: despite $\Kempirical{=}1$ on both C4 and Wikipedia, it yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv (1v2 $\auc = 0.820$, 2v3 $= 0.628$), and this profile persists under cross-scorer validation (Pythia-1.4B scorer: C4 $\Kempirical{=}1$, arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.608$, CI $[0.593, 0.622]$)), indicating that domain structural complexity can rescue even web-text one-wash generators (Table~\ref{tab:domain_kstar}).
Larger models produce more human-like text, reducing 0v1 JSD: 0.16--0.29 at $\leq$1.5B, 0.077--0.13 at 7--14B.
However, Qwen-14B still yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.621$, CI $[0.612, 0.630]$), with 0v1 $\auc = 0.960$ comparable to Qwen-7B ($0.964$), indicating that scale in the 1B--14B range does not shift the discrimination boundary.

\paragraph{Sample-size control.}
As a sample-size control, subsampling C4 to $n{=}2{,}200$ (matching arXiv) yields $\Kempirical{=}1$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.601$, CI $[0.586, 0.616]$).
Reduced sample size widens confidence intervals and produces more conservative $\Kempirical$ estimates, confirming that arXiv's $\Kempirical{=}3$ at the same sample size reflects genuine domain signal rather than a statistical artifact of smaller training sets.

